Lab 4: SQL Injection UNION Attack - Finding a Column
Containing Text
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab continues from the previous one. The product category filter is still vulnerable, and the results of
the query are shown back to us on the page. This time, instead of just counting columns, we need to find out
which one of those columns can actually hold text, so later we can use that column to pull out real data such as
usernames or passwords.

The lab gives us a random value that we must make appear somewhere on the page. If we succeed in making that
value show up, the lab considers it solved.

Goal: find which column in the query can hold text, by making a given string show up in the page results.

==============================
WHY WE NEED TO FIND A TEXT COLUMN

Not every column in a database table holds text. Some hold numbers, some hold dates, and some hold text. If we
want to use a UNION attack to steal actual text data later, such as usernames or passwords, we need to place that
text into a column that is capable of displaying text. If we try to place text into a column meant for numbers,
the database will throw an error and the UNION will fail.

So this lab is about testing each column one at a time, to see which one accepts a text value without causing an
error.

=============================
THE TECHNIQUE USED HERE

We already know the exact number of columns from the previous lab, because UNION attacks require both queries to
return the same number of columns.

Now, instead of filling every column with NULL, we fill all of them with NULL except one at a time. In that one
column, we place our target text value instead. We then check if that value appears correctly on the page. If it
does, we found a column that accepts text. If it causes an error or does not appear, we move the text to the
next column and try again.

======================================
WHAT THE PAYLOAD LOOKS LIKE

As an example, if there are three columns in total, and we are testing the second column, the payload would
look like: a valid category name, followed by a single quote, then UNION SELECT, then NULL, then our target text
surrounded by single quotes, then NULL again, then two dashes at the end.

Only one column at a time holds the text value, the rest remain NULL, so we can tell exactly which column
position is responsible if the value shows up.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and note the random target string the lab gives you.
Using the number of columns found earlier, submit a UNION SELECT where all columns are NULL except the first
one, which holds the target string in quotes.
If the page does not show the lab solved message, move the target string to the next column instead, keeping the
rest NULL.
Repeat this for each column position until the target string appears and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, used for sending web requests.

A session is created so cookies stay consistent across each test.

It asks you to paste the filter address, and saves it as filter_url.

It sets valid_category to the text Lifestyle, a real category name that exists in this lab, so the first part of
the original query still works normally.

It sets num_columns to three, which is the column count already discovered from the previous lab’s method.

It sets target_string to the random value given by the lab. This value is different for each person, since the
lab generates it uniquely per lab instance.

It then loops through each column position, starting from position zero up to the last column.

On each loop, it creates a list called row_layout, filled with the word NULL repeated once for every column.

It then replaces only the current column position in that list with the target string wrapped in single quotes,
leaving every other position as NULL.

It builds the full payload using the valid category name, a single quote, UNION SELECT, the row_layout values
joined together with commas, and two dashes at the end to comment out the rest of the original query.

This payload is sent as the category parameter to the website through the same session.

It checks if the response contains the sentence the lab shows when solved. If found, it prints a success
message showing which column position accepted the text, adding one to the position since humans usually count
columns starting from one instead of zero, and then stops the loop using break.

If none of the column positions work, the else part connected to the loop runs, printing a failure message
suggesting the column count or target string might be wrong.

=============================
WHY WE USE ZERO AS A STARTING POINT IN THE CODE

In Python, counting usually starts from zero instead of one. So the first column in the list is considered
position zero by the code, even though we normally call it column one when talking out loud. This is why the
script adds one to the column position before printing it, so the message makes sense to a human reading it.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is the same as before, user input is joined directly into the SQL query. This time the
UNION SELECT is being used not just to match column count, but to test which column can safely hold a text
value without causing a database type mismatch error.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so that no part of user input can ever be interpreted as SQL structure, including
UNION statements used for probing column types.

Other good practices:

Turn off detailed database error messages in production, since those errors reveal when a column type does not
match, which is exactly what attackers use to map out the table structure.
Apply strict input validation so attempts to inject quotes or SQL keywords are blocked before reaching the
database.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

Not all columns can hold text, some are built only for numbers or dates.
To find a text-compatible column, we test one column at a time with a text value while keeping the rest NULL.
If a column cannot hold text, the database usually throws an error instead of returning normal results.
This step builds directly on the column count found in the earlier lab, and it is often the next required step
before extracting real data in future labs.
